    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_WelcomeTitle">Welcome to vWheel Hub</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_WelcomePath">Your mouse and keyboard → a virtual racing controller</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_WelcomeBody">Thanks for downloading! Choose your language first. This short tour shows where to select devices, assign controls and get into a race. We will highlight real pages and explain common stumbling blocks.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_WelcomeTip">About 4 minutes. The hub stays stopped during the tour; no game settings or bindings are changed. You can reopen this guide from Getting started in the sidebar. Installed languages are listed above; missing translations use English.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ProfilesTitle">Start with a profile</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ProfilesPath">Profiles → game → profile</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ProfilesBody">A profile holds your steering, pedal and key settings. Choose the game and a profile here. Universal is a useful starting point for a game without a dedicated integration. You can create separate profiles for different cars or driving styles.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ProfilesTip">Check the profile name above Controls before editing. Game detection can suggest another context; review the switch prompt if you have unsaved changes.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_OutputTitle">What the game receives</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_OutputPath">Controls → Output Devices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_OutputBody">Change axis ranges and direction here. Device checks and repairs are available on Dashboard.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_OutputWindows">Windows uses vJoy. If setup is needed, Dashboard shows the problem and opens device repair. Configuration changes require confirmation.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_OutputLinux">Linux: use the native uinput output and grant the requested input/uinput access. Start the hub before the game. For LMU through Steam/Proton, disable Steam Input for the game so it can see the virtual wheel directly.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_InputTitle">Which devices you use</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_InputPath">Controls → Input devices</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_InputBody">Choose your mouse and keyboard in their cards, or identify them by movement and key press. Finding a device automatically stops the search. Use Save in the profile header to save the pair together with your other changes. The header shows when changes are unsaved.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_InputTip">Test selected devices only displays activity. Stopping a test keeps the draft. Undo in the header also reverts unsaved device selection. The saved mouse and keyboard remain shared by all profiles.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_KeysTitle">What each key does</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_KeysPath">Controls → Key bindings</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_KeysBody">Assign throttle, brake, gears, hub commands and virtual game buttons here. Click a key field and press the key or mouse button you want to use.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_KeysTip">There are two layers: assign your physical keys in the hub, then assign the hub’s virtual axes/buttons in the game. The next steps explain the second layer.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AssignmentTitle">Assign controls in the game</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AssignmentPath">Controls → In-game binding</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AssignmentBody">Choose steering, throttle, brake or a gear shift. Use Once for a single assignment so repeated presses cannot switch menus afterwards. Click Start, switch to the game and open the desired binding: the signal begins after 5 seconds.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AssignmentTip">Repeat runs until stopped. Escape or Stop ends the signal. Sound cues help you follow the countdown. Selecting physical devices on Input devices is a separate step; you do not need to know the underlying gamepad button name.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AxesTitle">Tune steering and pedals</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AxesPath">Controls → Axis controls → Steering / Gas / Brake</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AxesBody">Start with the defaults, then adjust one setting at a time. Steering controls how mouse movement or steering keys turn the wheel. Gas and Brake control how quickly virtual pedals press and release. Watch the output monitor while testing with the hub running, then check the result on track.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_AxesTip">Use the assignment tool when you need a known output signal. This tour never starts a signal or changes your bindings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ReadyTitle">Save, start, drive</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ReadyPath">Dashboard → start the hub</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ReadyBody">Save your profile before leaving. Start the hub, then launch the game and enter a race. Enable Lock Mouse when you want to steer without the pointer leaving the game; disable it to use the desktop again. You can assign hub and mouse-lock shortcuts on Key bindings.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ReadyTip">If something does not respond: check physical device activity → hub bindings → virtual output → game bindings. Reopen this guide anytime from Getting started. Finishing returns you to your previous page.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ShowPage">Show page</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_ShowExplanation">Show explanation</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_DashboardTitle">Your starting point</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_DashboardPath">Dashboard</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_DashboardBody">This is the first page you see. It shows the current game, hub status and device diagnostics. Start or stop controls with the hub switch above. The output monitor at the bottom shows steering, gas and brake. A connected controller and a connected game are separate things.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_DashboardTip">We will follow the sidebar and Controls tabs in their displayed order. The hub remains stopped throughout the tour.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_SettingsTitle">Application preferences</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_SettingsPath">Settings → General</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_SettingsBody">Here you configure how the application starts and behaves on the desktop: automatic hub startup, minimising, the system tray and mouse-lock sounds. Language can also be changed from the selector in the title bar. Set these preferences after your first working profile is ready.</sys:String>
    <sys:String x:Key="Tour_SettingsTip">The sidebar’s Getting started button reopens this tour whenever you need it. Skipping or finishing the tour saves that it has been shown.</sys:String>
